# Untitled

## A Catalytic Copper/Cobalt Oxide Interface for Efficient Hydrogen Generation
Metal nanoparticles and metal oxides promisingly provide different catalytic active sites at their interfaces. Constructing high-density interfaces is essential to maximize synergies. Herein, a \(\mathbf{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles interfacial structure produced via pyrolysis and moderate oxidation from metal-organic frameworks has been designed to boost the intrinsic activity. The \(\mathbf{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles composites exhibit a turnover frequency of \(57.5\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) for ammonia borane hydrolysis, far higher than those of monometallic Cu and \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles, showing the synergistic effect of Cu and \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles at their interface. Density functional theory calculations and in situ Raman spectroscopy reveal the catalytic mechanism of dual active sites, in which \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles at \(\mathbf{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) interface efficiently bind and activate water molecules and Cu nanoparticles easily activate \(\mathbf{NH}_{3}\mathbf{BH}_{3}\) molecules. This study opens up a new pathway for achieving high-efficiency noble metal-free catalysts for hydrogen generation and other heterogeneous catalysis.
## 1. Introduction
Hydrogen energy has attracted wide attention as a carrier of clean energy due to its utilization expected to solve the increasingly serious environmental and energy problems.[1,2] Ammonia borane \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\right)\) is non-toxicity, high hydrogen content \((19.6\%)\) and high stability at room temperature, recognized as one of the high-density chemical hydrogen storage materials.[3,4] High effective catalysts of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis for hydrogen generation often contain Pt, Pd, Ru, and Rh noble metals due to their suitable electron structure and
surface properties.[5–9] Considering their wide application, such as in three-way converters for cars, noble metal-based catalysts will have to face serious economic cost and resource scarcity.[10,11] For the smooth development of hydrogen energy in future, tremendous research efforts have been paid to explore non-noble metal catalysts as a substitution for noble metal.[12,13] Some elemental or compound materials containing the first row of transition metals, such as Co,[14,15] Ni,[16,17] Cu,[18,19] and Fe[20,21] have been used to catalyze the hydrolysis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\). In the current research progress, most non-noble metal-based catalysts only provide moderate catalytic performances for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis. The Co-based catalysts exhibit the most attractive intrinsic activity among these reported catalysts.[22] However, due to the irrational design strategies, the activity of the Co-based catalyst
is seriously influenced by catalyst composition, structure, interface, and the number and distribution of active sites. Up to date, the intrinsic activity of monometallic Co-based catalysts in \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis is still far from satisfying the requirements of practical applications.[23,24]
To substantially enhance the intrinsic activity of Co-based catalysts for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis, some bimetallic or polymetallic catalysts were prepared through adding the transition metals as a promoter to Co-based catalysts. These metals usually have a certain catalytic effects for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis, such as Cu, Fe, or Ni, which often form a positive synergy with cobalt sites, thus effectively enhancing the overall activity of the Co-based catalysts.[25,26] Corresponding to these advances in catalyst design and preparation, the catalytic mechanism also was clarified with several reaction pathways different with each other. CuCo bimetallic system shows a significant synergistic effect in \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis and displays a promising prospect in future application.[15,27] However, compared to noble metal-based catalysts, their catalytic activity still needs to be further improved through the rational design of bimetallic or polymetallic interface structure or the improvement of atomic utilization ratio under the guidance of clear mechanism understanding.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), owing to their large surface area and uniform porous structure created from supermolecular assembly of metal ions or clusters with organic ligands, can act as precursors or templates to a myriad of nanostructured materials with uniform metal sites.[28,29] The porous materials from MOFs precursors, which usually exhibit uniform and controllable distribution of active metal sites, large
specific surface area, and structure stability, have aroused considerable interests in various potential applications, such as catalysis, energy storage, and electrode materials for supercapacitors.[30–32] In order to maximize structure advantage and realize synergetic effect of multiple components, bimetal or trimetal organic frameworks are used as precursors for the fabrication of advanced functional materials.[33,34]
Herein, the interfaces of Cu NPs and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs ensemble \((\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN})\) were successfully synthesized through pyrolysis process followed by moderate oxidation from MOFs. The optimized catalyst of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) shows a high activity with a TOF value of \(37.3\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) and an excellent stability over five cycles during the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis. The synergy between Cu and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs at their interface leads to a much higher catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalyst than that of monometallic Cu NPs or \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs catalyst. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations and in situ Raman spectroscopy reveal the catalytic mechanism of dual active sites. Our research demonstrates the catalytic potential of dual active sites at the catalyst interface in a multi-molecular reaction system, and introduces a strategy for developing non-noble metal catalysts.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Catalysts Characterization
The \(\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalysts of various copper-cobalt ratios with high density \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface were synthesized through high-temperature calcination of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{MOF}\) at \(550\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in nitrogen followed by activation at \(200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in air for \(22\ \mathrm{h}\) (Figure 1a). Characterization of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) using powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) is exhibited in Figure 1b. The peaks located at \(36.6^{\circ}\), \(59.6^{\circ}\), and \(64.9^{\circ}\) are related to the (311), (511), and (440) lattice facets of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\),[35] as well as the peaks at \(43.5^{\circ}\) and \(44.6^{\circ}\) correspond to crystal planes of metallic Cu (111) and Co (002), respectively.[36,37] A small amount of Co is present because Co at the center of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) nanoparticles (NPs) has not been oxidized. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) analysis show clearly that Cu nanocrystals with an average size of \(7.0\ \mathrm{nm}\) are surrounded by \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanocrystals with an average size of \(5.3\ \mathrm{nm}\), and the crystal boundaries between them are visible (Figure 1c,d, and Figure S1, Supporting Information). In the HRTEM image, the d-spacing of \(0.208\ \mathrm{nm}\) is assigned to Cu (111) plane, and \(0.243\ \mathrm{nm}\) to \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (311), indicating the existence of crystalline phase in Cu and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) regions. The planes of Cu (111) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (311) were also observed in the TEM and HRTEM images of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (Figure S2, Supporting Information), indicating no alloying and chemical reaction between copper and cobalt elements during the pyrolysis of CuCo-MOF. Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) mappings and EDX spectra confirm the uniform distribution of Cu, Co, C, O, and N elements in catalyst (Figure 1e and Figure S3 and S4, Supporting Information). Scanning electron microscopic (SEM) images show two block shapes of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (311), and a spherical shape of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) between 20 and \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) in size (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The metal loading and exact atomic ratios in series \(\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalysts are further determined by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (Table S1, Supporting Information).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis (Figure 2) of Cu 2p and Co 2p regions of the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalyst suggest that
copper element is mainly present in \(\mathrm{Cu}^{0}\) (932.8 and \(952.7\ \mathrm{eV}\)), and a small amount of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)oxidation state (934.6 and \(954.5\ \mathrm{eV}\)) originates from surface oxidation. Cobalt element is in \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) (780.1 and \(795.1\ \mathrm{eV}\)) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) oxidation state (781.6 and \(796.6\ \mathrm{eV}\)). Compared to corresponding monometallic compounds \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\) and \(\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\), the binding energies of Cu 2p and Co 2p have basically no shift after the formation of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) (Figure S6, Supporting Information), indicating no intrinsic charge redistribution between copper and cobalt elements during the formation of \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface.[38] This fact provides solid evidence that the synergy of copper and cobalt does not derive from the modulation of their electronic structure. The surface compositions of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\), \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\), and \(\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\) show that C 1s, N 1s, O 1s Co 2p, and Cu 2p exist in corresponding catalysts with the binding energies of 285.5, 399.9, 532.0, 782.5, and \(933.4\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The elemental contents from XPS are listed in Table S2, Supporting Information. The N element originates from doped chitosan. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption isotherms of \(\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\), \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\), and \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) samples are shown in Figure S8, Supporting Information. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of the \(\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\), \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\), and \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) samples are 132.2, 1.0, and \(51.3\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. And their structures include micropores and mesopores with an average pore width of 6.35, 8.35, and \(8.46\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively. Most of the copper element in the \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\) sample exists in the form of copper NPs, resulting in a small BET surface area.
## 2.2. Catalytic Performance
The activity of various samples was evaluated using the hydrolysis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\). The specific rate and TOF curve of hydrogen generation catalyzed by \(\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalysts at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) are presented in Figure 3a,b. The rates of hydrogen generation increase first and then decrease with the increasing of Cu proportion (Figure 3a). The TOF values increase from 1.4 \((\mathrm{CuO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN})\) to \(57.5\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) \((\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN})\), then decrease to \(7.15\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) \((\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN})\) (Figure 3b). Monometallic catalysts of \(\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\) and \(\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\) show low catalytic activity and the presence of copper in \(\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\) significantly increase the catalyst activity. All the \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) composite catalysts give higher catalytic activity than the monometallic catalysts of \(\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\) and \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}@\mathrm{CN}\), suggesting the synergistic effect between \(\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs. Table S3, Supporting Information, shows the TOF values of some representative monometallic Cu or Co, and bimetallic \(\mathrm{CuCo}\) catalysts, which demonstrates the outstanding synergy between Cu and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs in the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis.
