## 2.3. Effect of Activation
To investigate the effect of moderate activation on catalyst activity in the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis, the performance and structure of catalyst before and after activation \((\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}@\mathrm{CN}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalysts) were investigated (Figure 4). Compared with \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}@\mathrm{CN}\), the catalytic rate of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) increases obviously and its TOF value increase from 27.5 to \(57.5~\mathrm{min}^{-1}\), indicating moderate activation is beneficial to boost the catalyst activity (Figure 4a). The HRTEM image (Figure 4b) shows that Cu nanocrystal exists in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalyst. Characterization of the catalysts by PXRD (Figure 4c) shows
copper element exists in the form of Cu crystal before and after activation, whereas most of cobalt element is oxidized from Co to \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) after activation. In addition, XPS analysis of the Cu 2p shows that its binding energy has almost no shift before and after activation, implying the copper element is mainly present in the state of \({\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}\) in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CN}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalysts (Figures 2a and 4d and Figure S13a, Supporting Information). XPS analysis of Co 2p regions shows that its binding energy increases \(2.45~\mathrm{eV}\) after activation, revealing that cobalt element is oxidized (Figures 2b and 4e and
Figure S13b, Supporting Information). This is supported by XPS analysis of O 1s (Figure 4f). The O 1s analysis of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}@\mathrm{CN}\) suggests that oxygen element is present in three states: \(\mathrm{C-O/O-H}\), \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) from the ligand of MOF, and \(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{O}\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co},\mathrm{Cu})\), corresponding binding energies of 533.7, 532.2, and \(530.1~\mathrm{eV}\), respectively.[41] Compared with \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalyst, the increasing of peak area of \(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{O}\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co},\mathrm{Cu})\) in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}@\mathrm{CN}\) catalyst indicates that the metal was oxidized in the process of activation. The binding energies of \(\mathrm{C-O/O-H}\) and \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) decreasing by 0.6 and \(0.5~\mathrm{eV}\) after activation, respectively, is contrary to the shift of Co 2p, proposing charge redistribution between Co and oxygen elements in the activation process.[25] The N 1s peaks of the catalysts before and after activation (Figure S14a, Supporting Information) were similar, broken down into pyrrolic nitrogen \((399.0~\mathrm{eV})\) and graphitic nitrogen \((401.0~\mathrm{eV})\).[42] The similar XPS spectra of C 1s before and after activation show no change in electronic structure (Figure S14b, Supporting Information). The analysis above suggests that the formation of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and the synergistic effects between \(\mathrm{Cu}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) are beneficial to improve the catalyst activity for the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis.
## 2.4. Catalytic Mechanism Analysis
The DFT simulation calculations were employed to explore the possible mechanism of \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanocrystals as a highly active catalyst and the synergistic mode of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (Figure 5a–f).[43,44] The free energy changes were listed in Table S4, Supporting Information. There is no energy barrier in adsorption step of two molecules, but there is an obvious energy barriers in dissociation process. At the \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface, the reaction barrier (Table S4, Supporting Information) of
water molecule at Co site \((0.60~\mathrm{eV})\) is lower than that at Cu site \((0.80~\mathrm{eV})\), while \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) molecule at Cu site \((0.17~\mathrm{eV})\) is lower than that at Co site \((0.21~\mathrm{eV})\). In other words, the Co sites at \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface could efficiently activate water molecule, and Cu sites could successful activate \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) molecule. The abundant \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interfaces of \(\mathrm{CuCoO@CN}\) make the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis reaction easier owing to the synergy between \(\mathrm{Cu}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\). For monometallic catalyst, \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) surface needs high energy to dissociate \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) molecule, and \(\mathrm{Cu}\) NP fails to activate water molecule. The interface between \(\mathrm{Cu}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) can assist the \(\mathrm{CuCoO@CN}\) catalyst to reduce the activation energy barriers of both water and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) molecules at the same time, which promote the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis and improve the catalyst activity. Detailed reaction pathways of adsorption and dissociation processes of water and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) molecules at \(\mathrm{Cu}\) site (Cu and \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface) and Co site \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface) were described (Figure 5d,e).
To gain a deeper understanding of the activity of \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface catalysts, the projected density of states (PDOS) around the Fermi level were calculated. The PDOS analysis of \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface reveals a remarkably high electron density of states (Figure 5f) at Fermi level compared with monometallic \(\mathrm{Cu}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\). Such electron density can decrease the reactants adsorption energy and lead to outstanding activity for \(\mathrm{CuCoO@CN}\) catalysts.[45–47] The differential charge density (Figure 5c) shows a electron accumulation (yellow) at \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) region and depletion (blue) at \(\mathrm{Cu}\) region between \(\mathrm{Cu}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface, further providing visual evidence that constructing the \(\mathrm{Cu-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) interface could effectively improve catalyst activity.
The previous mechanism research of the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis suggests that the cleavage of \(\mathrm{O-H}\) bond of water molecule is the rate-determining step and the catalyst activity may be improved by
increasing the adsorption and dissociation of water molecule on the catalyst surface.[39,43,48,49] In situ, Raman analysis was used to investigate the real behavior of water molecule on catalyst interface. The Raman spectra of \(\mathrm{H-O-H}\) stretching and bending mode of interfacial water are exhibited in Figure 5g,h. The peak at \(1590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is assigned to the \(\mathrm{H}-\) \(\mathrm{O-H}\) bending vibration and the peaks at 3392 and \(3235~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) to the \(\mathrm{H-O-H}\) stretching vibration of trihedrally and tetrahedrally coordinated water on the catalysts interface.[50–52] Figure 5g,h show that the adsorbed water has Raman signals, while pure water does not. The strongest adsorption of water molecule occurs at the \({\mathrm{Cu-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) surface, followed by the \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) surface, then the copper surface, which is consistent with the catalysts activity. In addition, the peak at \(1590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) of Raman spectrum of water molecules on \({\mathrm{Cu-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) surface is splitted and appears a low wave number peak at \(1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) due to Fermi resonance. The \(\mathrm{H-O-H}\) bond energy is partially weakened and easy to dissociate, which would be beneficial to the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis and improving the catalyst activity according to the mechanism of the rate-determining step.[48,49] This experimental observation presents a key evidence for the DFT investigation on the high activity origin of \(\mathrm{Cu}-\) \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) catalysts.
## 3. Conclusions
In conclusion, we have proposed a method for the fabrication of \(\mathrm{Cu}-\) \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) interface catalyst with high-density active sites by pyrolysis and moderate oxidation from MOFs. The \({\mathrm{Cu-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) composites as catalyst for the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis show high catalytic activity and stability. The optimum \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{O@CN}\) catalyst achieves a high activity with a TOF \(57.7~\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) , far higher than monometallic \({\mathrm{CoO}}_{\mathrm{x}}@{\mathrm{CN}}\) and \({\mathrm{CuO}}_{\mathrm{x}}@{\mathrm{CN}}\) . DFT calculations show that the \({\mathrm{Cu-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) interface could reduce the activation energy of two molecules, where Cu could
effectively activate \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) molecule and \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) activate water molecule, and the synergy of dual active sites of \({\mathrm{Cu-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) facilitates the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\) hydrolysis. In situ Raman analysis supplies a key evidence for the DFT investigation on the high activity origin of \({\mathrm{Cu-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) catalysts. This research illustrates the potential of using the metal-metal oxide interface structure in multi-molecular reaction systems, and introduces a strategy for developing non-noble metal catalysts.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate \((\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) , 1,3,5-benzeretricarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}\right)\) , glacial acetic acid \(\left(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O H}\right)\) , and methanol were supplied by Aladdin Chemicals (Shanghai, China). Copper nitrate trihydrate \((\mathsf{C u}\) \((\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})\) , N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), chitosan, and \(\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{B H}_{3}\) were obtained from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). All reagents used in this study are analytical grade.
Synthesis of catalysts: \(\mathsf{C u}_{\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{M O F}\) . These series samples of \(\mathsf{C u}_{x}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{M O F}\) were prepared through solvothermal method. Typically, a methanol solution \((20\ m L)\) containing \(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((0.522\ g\ 1.216\ m m o l)\) and \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((0.419\ g\ 1.44\ m m o l)\) was poured into a DMF solution \((20\ m L)\) containing \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}\) \((0.840\ g\ 2.0\ m m o l)\) . After 30 min of ultrasonication, the mixture was added to a water solution \((20\ m L)\) containing chitosan \((0.50\ g)\) and glacial acetic acid \((0.5\ m L)\) , and then sonicated for \(1\ h\) . The suspension was heated at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\ h\) , Teflon-lined autoclave \((150\ m L)\) . The powder was centrifuged and washed with DMF, methanol and water for three times, respectively. The \(\mathsf{C u}_{0.6}\mathsf{C o}_{0.4}\mathsf{M O F}\) sample was obtained after drying at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\ h\) . Cu-MOF, Co-MOF, \(\mathsf{C u}_{0.2}\mathsf{C o}_{0.8}\mathsf{M O F},\) \(\mathsf{C u}_{0.4}\mathsf{C o}_{0.6}\mathsf{M O F},\) and \(\mathsf{C u}_{0.8}\mathsf{C o}_{0.2}\mathsf{M O F}\) were also prepared through changing the Cu/ Co molar ratio.
\(\mathsf{C u}_{x}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{O C N}\) and \(\mathsf{C u}_{x}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{O@CN}\) . The precursors \(\mathsf{C u}_{x}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{M O F}\) were heated up to \(550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rate of \(5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) and kept for 1 h under \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) atmosphere in quartz tube. Dark brown \(\mathsf{C u}_{x}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{O C N}\) were obtained after slowly cooling. Note that the dark brown powder must be first immersed into ethanol under nitrogen protection due to its combustion spontaneous in air atmosphere. Then, \(\mathsf{C u}_{x}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{O@CN}\) products were obtained after \(\mathsf{C u}_{x}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{O C N}\) samples were activated at \(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in air for \(22\ h\) .
Characterization: The micro morphology of sample was characterized using JSM-6700F SEM (JEOL Ltd., Japan). TEM was performed on a FEI Tecnai \(C^{2}\) F20 electron microscope, operating at \(200~\mathsf{k V}.\) The phase structure of sample \((30~\mathsf{m g})\) was determined with X-ray diffraction (XRD, PANalytical X'Pert PRO, Cu \(\mathsf{K}\alpha=\) \(1.3416\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}_{\mathsf{r}})\) over the angular range from \(4^{\circ}\) to \(80^{\circ}\) . XPS was done on a PHI quantera SXM spectrometer with an Al \(\mathsf{K}\alpha=1486.60~\mathsf{e V}\) excitation source, and the binding energies were calibrated by referencing the C 1s peak \((284.8~\mathsf{e V})\) to reduce the sample charge effect. The specific surface area of the sample was measured with nitrogen adsorption isotherms on a specific surface analyzer (ASAP 2420, Micrometrics, USA). In situ Raman spectrum of sample was collected on Renishaw RM-2000 with an excitation wavelength of \(514~\mathsf{n m}\) . Raman spectrum of pure water was detected on the sheet of \(\mathsf{S i O}_{2}\) . The details of DFT simulation are in the Supporting Information.
Catalytic hydrolysis of \(N H_{3}B H_{3}\) : The \(\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{B H}_{3}\) hydrolysis catalyzed by \(\mathsf{C u}_{x}\mathsf{C o}_{1-x}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C N}\) series catalysts was carried out in a \(50~\mathsf{m L}\) of round bottom flask fixed in a magnetic constant temperature water bath. Typically, \(10~\mathsf{m g}\) of catalyst was added into the flask, then \(5~\mathsf{m L}\) of \(\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{B H}_{3}\) \((38~\mathsf{m g})\) aqueous solution was rapidly injected using a syringe. The reaction was performed under magnetic
syring at \(500~\mathsf{r p m}\) , \(25~^{\circ}\subset.\) The produced hydrogen was collected using water displacement method, and its volume was equal to that of the displaced water. The hydrogen generation specific rate \((r_{\mathsf{B}})\) was computed as following formula (Equation 1):
\[
r_{\mathrm{B}}=\frac{40(\mathsf{mL})}{\left[t_{60}-t_{20}\right]\cdot w_{c}(\mathsf{g})}
\]
where \(t_{60}\) represents the time for \(60~\mathsf{m L}\) of hydrogen evolution, \(t_{20}\) for \(20~\mathsf{m L}\) , and \(w_{c}\) is the Co weight in catalyst.
Turnover frequency (TOF, \(\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) ) is used to evaluate the catalyst activity, and its value was computed as following formula:
\[
\mathsf{TOF}=\frac{n_{\mathsf{H}_2}}{n_{\mathsf{Co}}t}
\]
where \(n_{\mathsf{H}_{2}}\) is the mole of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\) produced during \(20{-}60~\mathsf{m L}\) , \(n_{\mathsf{C o}}\) represents the total mole of Co in catalysts. \(t\) is the reaction time during \(20{-}60~\mathsf{m L}\) . The
values of TOF and \( r_{\mathsf{B}} \) are calculated without considering Cu content (Equations 1 and 2) because the monometallic catalytic activity of \(\mathsf{C u O}_{x}\mathsf{@C N}\) is extremely low compared with \(\mathsf{C o O}_{x}\mathsf{@C N}\).
